  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64040,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Clinical features include abortion and abnormal antenatal ultrasound findings {11603213}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64041,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64042,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Known causes include trisomy syndromes and non-molar triploidy. In many cases, the etiology is unknown.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64043,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Abnormal (non-molar) villous lesions have diverse origins related to the specific diagnosis causing the abnormal villous morphology. Various chromosomal or genetic alterations may be found {30952972}. Hydropic (non-molar) abortions and chromosomal trisomy syndromes are often responsible for atypical/dysmorphic villous morphology. Less common causes include digynic triploid conception and placental mesenchymal dysplasia {23518914}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64044,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>They vary from normal gross findings to discernible vesicle formation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64045,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Chorionic villi often display some degree of irregularity in size and shape, with enlargement and focal mild trophoblastic proliferation (sometimes manifesting as syncytiotrophoblastic snouts) and occasional trophoblastic pseudoinclusions. This atypical/dysmorphic villous morphology shares features within the spectrum of the histological alterations of PHM. Consequently, the morphological diagnosis of PHM is imprecise. Placental mesenchymal dysplasia, which is characterized by stem villi cystic dilatation, is usually seen later in gestation and is associated with intrauterine growth restriction, non-molar mosaic conceptions, and Beckwith&ndash;Wiedemann syndrome {25171710}. p57 is expressed in cytotrophoblast and villous stromal cells in both PHM and these conditions (with the exception of Beckwith&ndash;Wiedemann syndrome). Ki-67 immunostaining cannot reliably distinguish PHM from non-molar abortion {30394942}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64046,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64047,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DNA genotyping analysis effectively distinguishes these lesions from PHM and may detect aneuploid conceptuses {20308840; 27636887}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64048,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> abnormal villous features that overlap with those of partial mole.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64049,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64050,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 276,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are non-molar entities and therefore have the same low risk of persistent gestational trophoblastic disease as any other morphologically unremarkable non-molar conception. When definitive genotyping studies are not available, women in whom PHM is suspected because of the presence of atypical/dysmorphic villous morphology may be monitored by hCG surveillance {25078332}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:51:45"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]